// The stages package: layer 3 of Cone's streaming design, stages between actors.
// A stream between actors is a run of owned chunks (an IoBuf each), handed on by
// a call and given back by another, under credit; each stage is an actor over a
// transform of package streams (layer 1) that has no I/O in it.
//
//   mod squash;
//   import actors;
//   import deflate;
//   import stages use *;
//
//   // sink end first: each stage is made with the one after it
//   let inf = TransformStage[deflate.Inflater](sink, deflate.Inflater.make(deflate.Format.Gzip));
//   source.start(inf);                 // a source that sends chunks to 'inf'
//
// WHAT IS HERE
//
//   Ending         how a stream ended: Finished, or Failed(AnyError) (transformstage.cone)
//   ChunkSink      an actor trait: accept(c), end(how), attach(up)
//   ChunkSource    an actor trait: giveBack(b), the credit coming back
//   DigestSink[Out] an actor trait: digest(r), where a hash stage sends its digest (Ok)
//                  or the failure that ended the stream (Err): one answer, always
//   ChunkWindow    a stream's window of bytes in flight, its buffers from a ChunkPool: the
//                  credit, the sending half (chunkwindow.cone)
//   ChunkPool      the buffers a connection or pipeline may hold, a cap shared by its
//                  links (chunkpool.cone)
//   ChunkInbox, Turn  the receiving half of a link, in a sink whose 'accept' awaits: keeps
//                  one stream's chunks in order and its 'end' after the last (chunkinbox.cone)
//   ReaderSink[R]  a generic actor, the sink an actor reads: holds each chunk until a
//                  'readSink(&rs)' has read it through, then gives it back; the answer R is
//                  made by two functions given at construction (readersink.cone)
//   ValueSink[T], ValueSource  the second family, for a stream of VALUES (any Sendable T) rather than
//                  bytes: accept(v), end(how), attach(up) and credit(n), stop(why); credit is a
//                  count the sink grants as it uses items; the contract, control items included, is
//                  written in valuesink.cone
//   ValueReader[T, R]  a generic actor, the value twin of ReaderSink: holds each item until a
//                  'readValue(&vr)' takes it, granting credit as items are taken (valuesink.cone)
//   TransformStage[T]  a generic actor: a stage over any Transform T, one that makes new
//                  chunks. The codecs are the instances:
//                    TransformStage[deflate.Inflater](down, inflater)     inflate a gzip, zlib or raw deflate stream
//                    TransformStage[deflate.Deflater](down, deflater)     compress into one
//                    TransformStage[zstd.Decompressor](down, decompressor)  decompress zstd frames
//                    TransformStage[zstd.Compressor](down, compressor)    compress into a zstd frame
//                  (the transform is made first, with its own 'make', and handed in)
//   HashStage[D]   a generic actor: a stage over any Digester D, passing the chunks on
//                  unchanged and sending the digest of all of them to 'to', a
//                  DigestSink[D.Out], when the stream finishes:
//                    HashStage[cryptohash.Sha256](down, sha256, to)   to: DigestSink[Array[u8, 32]]
//                    HashStage[cryptohash.Sha512](down, sha512, to)   to: DigestSink[Array[u8, 64]]
//                    HashStage[cryptohash.Sha1](down, sha1, to)       to: DigestSink[Array[u8, 20]]
//
// transformstage.cone is the longer account: the end and an error travel forward as the
// call 'end(how)', credit is a window of bytes and memory a pool, and a pipeline is
// wired from the sink end back. A corrupt gzip stream reaches the last sink as
// 'Ending.Failed(e)', with the inflater's own message and its DeflateError inside
// the AnyError.
//
// A SINK THAT AWAITS inside 'accept' (to write a file, say) is parked there and
// the actor runs its next message meanwhile, so the next chunk, or the 'end' that
// follows the last, can start before that chunk is finished. Such a sink keeps an
// ChunkInbox per stream (chunkinbox.cone): it queues what arrives while a chunk is in hand,
// gives each buffer back only when its chunk is finished, and lets the 'end' out
// after the last. A stage that never awaits needs none.
//
// The stages are generic actors: 'TransformStage[T: Transform]' is one declaration, and
// each T it is made at is an actor of its own, with its own state, messages and
// handle. Layer 3 is here and not in package streams so that the codecs, which
// import streams, do not import actors.
//
// 'down <- chunk' is 'down.accept(chunk)': ChunkSink's accept declares '<-' as its overload name.

mod stages;

import iobuf use IoBuf;
import collections use AnyError, List, Deque, String;
import actors;
import streams use *;
import sync use Arc;

// How far ahead a stream's link may run by default (its window, in bytes), and the
// size a stage makes its chunks at most
pub const linkWindow = 262144usize;
pub const linkBytes = 65536usize;
